#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172135 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "355c5dfd-d06f-56ef-9c38-8dccf702467e" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14de2753-c73d-55f7-8bc7-5fae82ecc1e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6y\\,$ and $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512143} {:answer/id #uuid "4dbe397f-fccb-5393-89f9-49deddcc70fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3y\\,$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512267} {:answer/id #uuid "91d37c9a-fa0d-55c8-8aed-0c8bcbdb8e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3{y}^{2}\\,$ and $y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512445} {:answer/id #uuid "be7ff705-7435-5e6b-8ed0-6f09f99cd7f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6y\\,$ and $y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512556} {:answer/id #uuid "e65e780e-ffad-5628-b3c2-57fcf167b02c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3{y}^{2}\\,$ and $z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14de2753-c73d-55f7-8bc7-5fae82ecc1e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6y\\,$ and $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512143} :answer-field/id #uuid "638d1388-9a4e-5ae4-8bcd-3602b1fb5c0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "773e8a5f-acc5-5d6b-95d3-53e47a7f0361" :question/math-academy-id "q-162671" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x+{y}^{2}+z$ on the intersection of the surfaces $3{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2$ and $x+{z}^{2}=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨1,\\,2y,\\,1⟩=\\lambda ⟨0,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨1,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,2z⟩ \\\\ 3{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2 \\\\ x+{z}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x+{y}^{2}+z\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=3{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2,\\,h(x,y,z)=x+{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=2 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,\\,2y,\\,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨0,\\,6y,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨1,\\,0,\\,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401391 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172135 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "355c5dfd-d06f-56ef-9c38-8dccf702467e" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b59bf81-336b-5b77-a5ab-993e5da369cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401393} {:answer/id #uuid "cd2b1eac-ff7d-50ba-8e40-931f60aee6f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401394} {:answer/id #uuid "372a272b-dcaf-5ac5-a75d-2792619b147e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2x$ and $2y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401395} {:answer/id #uuid "92eb8681-34a4-5d3f-9b19-cabe81d62073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2x$ and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401396} {:answer/id #uuid "eda1895b-7d76-519b-8bab-824851119ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "372a272b-dcaf-5ac5-a75d-2792619b147e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2x$ and $2y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401395} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ec7c466-5f47-5c18-934e-2ba7c46ca3fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8afa6e7b-08b3-5fd6-b960-921c9d4bc534" :question/math-academy-id "q-162811" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z$ on the intersection of the surfaces ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ and ${y}^{2}+z=9$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨2x,\\,2yz,\\,{y}^{2}⟩=\\lambda ⟨\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,0,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨0,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,1⟩ \\\\ {x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1 \\\\ {y}^{2}+z=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+z=9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,\\,2yz,\\,{y}^{2}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,\\,0,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨0,\\,2y,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172135 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "355c5dfd-d06f-56ef-9c38-8dccf702467e" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a26a345-848a-52d6-9cff-309d48842caa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z\\,$ and $2x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401400} {:answer/id #uuid "0336a2fb-21ec-5932-b4bf-96df7edcc264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\,$ and $x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401401} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3832c6-972f-5f02-873d-640f27294ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,$ and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401402} {:answer/id #uuid "f73da70f-25e8-5f76-94d0-6c2f5b70918f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401403} {:answer/id #uuid "13062583-c8cd-58e7-92bd-33acf71104ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\,$ and $-y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d3832c6-972f-5f02-873d-640f27294ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,$ and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401402} :answer-field/id #uuid "6944c355-576e-5c8e-b344-9b3d5fd644ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a114a88-7824-55e2-8ac4-08f991d5f004" :question/math-academy-id "q-162812" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=xy+{z}^{2}$ on the intersection of the surfaces $y-x=0$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨y,\\,x,\\,2z⟩=\\lambda ⟨-1,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,0⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ y-x=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+{z}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=y-x=0,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=0 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨y,\\,x,\\,2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,0⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172135 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "355c5dfd-d06f-56ef-9c38-8dccf702467e" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bf5cbc8-973c-579a-9b90-abbe4012f724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3x,\\,$ $-1,\\,$ and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512124} {:answer/id #uuid "15eb0be1-2146-557a-bf30-037c1787fc52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3z-{y}^{2},\\,$ $1,\\,$ and $4z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512149} {:answer/id #uuid "774b441c-9612-5738-9b25-33d37aa3bb97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3z,\\,$ $-1,\\,$ and $4z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512381} {:answer/id #uuid "7973a813-a61d-52de-b2c1-a38a665ca2d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z,\\,$ $1,\\,$ and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512386} {:answer/id #uuid "ec27c4f6-6609-57e9-84da-6d70e103712d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3z,\\,$ $0,\\,$ and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "774b441c-9612-5738-9b25-33d37aa3bb97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3z,\\,$ $-1,\\,$ and $4z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512381} :answer-field/id #uuid "0df0304e-ec0c-5d9e-8ca3-8b7c953f670a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9abfcd2-1f16-5a00-8346-efd5d81231cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-162817" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the minimum of the function $f(x,y,z)=3xz+{y}^{2}$ on the intersection of the surfaces $x-y=1$ and $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}=4$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system:  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,2y,\\,3x⟩=\\lambda ⟨1,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,0\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨4x,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ x-y=1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=3xz+{y}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x-y=1,\\,h(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨3z,\\,2y,\\,3x⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩+\\mu ⟨4x,\\,2y,\\,4z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172134 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "496fa617-6592-57af-89ac-b77d68df0bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35746dd5-60c7-5b7c-9936-da62f086bab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512218} {:answer/id #uuid "88b5f66c-f0e6-5312-b69e-25574fe5c5f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512418} {:answer/id #uuid "da111704-a982-535c-b453-0caf2ce1247c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512619} {:answer/id #uuid "f07b1405-5db5-53fd-92b4-e9d3aa736944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512682} {:answer/id #uuid "f4740cdc-4247-561b-a719-1d4642b2932f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4740cdc-4247-561b-a719-1d4642b2932f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512691} :answer-field/id #uuid "f02d209a-9adb-5732-8214-dc18cb0b1819" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3250257a-998e-56c2-b2b7-fdd8d8e6863b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162896" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x+y-2z$ on the intersection of the plane $x+y+4z=4$ and the cylinder $4{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x+y-2z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x+y+4z=4,\\,h(x,y,z)=4{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=4 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,4⟩+\\mu ⟨8x,0,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}1=\\lambda +8\\mu x \\\\ 1=\\lambda  \\\\ -2=4\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\mu x=0 \\\\ \\lambda =1 \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{\\mu }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction. So, from the first equation above, we must have $x=0.$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=0$  into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ z & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $z$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=0$ and $z=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+4z & =4 \\\\ 0+y+4(1) & =4 \\\\ y+4 & =4 \\\\ y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=0$ and $z=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+4z & =4 \\\\ 0+y+4(-1) & =4 \\\\ y-4 & =4 \\\\ y & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0,1),\\,(0,8,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(0,0,1)$ | $(0,8,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $-2$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,8,-1),$ and its  maximum value is $10.$"}]}
